/* ================================================================
   Sound: everything is synthesized with Web Audio (no files).
   Cannon booms, shell whooshes, splashes, explosions, the ocean, rain, engines.
   Sounds are panned by where they happen on screen and fade when far off-screen.
   ================================================================ */
const SFX = { ctx: null, master: null, noise: null, brown: null, whoosh: [], amb: null, eng: [] };
/* (Nov 10, the iPad: "Failed to start the audio device", and no sound after it) the sound is started and kept going by one rule.
   Safari lets sound start only inside a user's own touch, key or click, and stops a context on its own: an interruption (a call,
   Siri, another app's sound, the audio session taken) leaves it 'interrupted', the page sent behind leaves it 'suspended' (we
   suspend it ourselves - 20-input-pointer.js), and a resume() it can't honour - outside a touch, or while the device won't start -
   is refused by rejecting its promise (InvalidStateError "Failed to start the audio device"). So every touch, key and click
   (audioInit, below: listened for before the game's own handlers) wakes the sound: no context yet - it's made; one that isn't
   running - it's resumed there, a silent sound played in the same handler (Safari's unlock); a refused resume is noted and tried
   again at the next touch, never left as an uncaught rejection. Only a closed context (it can never run again) is replaced, with
   a new one built as the first was. Each change of its state is noted in the log the REPORT button saves (50-zzzz-bug.js) */
/* (user Nov 11 09:38: "if I'm playing the game on Safari and I alt tab to another application, it will then lose the sound when I return")
   WebKit's context, back from behind another app, can say 'running' and play nothing, or stay stopped through every resume. So a page
   that was sent behind (or a window left while its context stopped) has its sound built again from nothing at the first touch, key or
   click after it's back - a new context, inside that touch, as the first one was; elsewhere (Chrome, Firefox) only when it isn't running */
